A||||8||GT Bastard||V6 guru's, help a fellow nut!||||9
Z||000000||GT Bastard||09-27-1999||04:42 PM||||Hey guys... When I start up my 87 GT cold in the morning, it sounds kindof like it's missing on one cylinder.  It sometimes tries to die unless I rev it a little, and the idle sounds lumpy.  After it's warmed up, it runs like a dream, with just a little bit of idle floating(about 50 rpm, which is about normal right?).  It's got new plugs and wires, fuel filter, air filter, and I just cleaned the throttle body real good.  What does everybody think?  <P>-Jeff||||reg||9
Z||000001||WisconsinGT||09-27-1999||06:33 PM||||Damn Jeff, I was going to ask the same question. Ever since the temp has dropped, my 88GT does not want to idle at start-up. But once it is warm, all is well.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||09-27-1999||08:56 PM||||Hey, maybe we should join forces and go on a crusade to try and correct the problem!  Could it have something to do with the IAC?  What have you done to correct the problem on YOU car?||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||09-27-1999||10:51 PM||||Disconnect the electrical connector to the Cold Start Injector switch (In the intake manifold).<P>The crank time will increase, but see if it runs better when you first start it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JetroGT||09-28-1999||12:13 AM||||My car does this as well. I have replaced everyting except the coil. When it idles it sounds like its missing. With 121,000 miles it's either that or a worn cam.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Speadraser||09-29-1999||12:42 PM||||To correctly isolate the IAC turn the key to "on" without starting, diconnect the IAC.  Start the engine.  If the idle is smooth then the IAC isn't compensating quick enough to maintain a smooth idle.  Replace the IAC.  If the idle remains unstable albeit unusally high or low then the IAC is working properly, and the problem lies somewhere else.  <P>Terryk the cold start injector is only enabled during the start mode.  After the key returns to the "on" position, engine running, the cold start injector is disabled.  <P>The problem could lie with the coolant temp sensor.  I have a chart that breaks down temp sensor resistances vs. temps if somebody wanted to remove their temp sensor and check it's accuracy.  A cooking thermometer, a multimeter, and a pan full of water on a range oven could help you fix your problem.  Another thing I just though of, the coolant sensor may have a buildup of minerals on it.  These minerals may act as a insulator on the actual probe and prevent the ECM in determining changes in coolant temp.  This would definately present a driveability problem.  The engine would run rough until the surrounding metal around the sensor and the rest of the engine reached operating temp.  These mineral deposits could be prevented if distilled water was used to cut the antifreeze instead of tap water.<BR>Speady<p>[This message has been edited by Speadraser (edited 09-29-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||mwbackus||09-29-1999||12:53 PM||||Sounds alot like clogged injector(s) to me.  This would cause a lean mix at startup and cause most of what was described.  After warm up (closed loop), the ECM would compensate for the clogged injectors by using addtional injector pulses.  The motor would seem to run ok, but would actually be lacking a considerable amount of power.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||09-29-1999||02:56 PM||||I believe the circuit is a combination of time and coolant temp. 8 seconds and/or 116F I believe. I believe that starting the car does not immediately shuts off the CSI.<P>Is it powered through START/CRANK power only? I though it was hot in run too. I'll look at the schematics. On the L44's sister engine the LB6, it's hot in run and crank.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||GT Bastard||09-30-1999||05:06 PM||||Thanks MwBackus, Terryk, and Speadraser... I'll try some of that stuff and see what happens.  Maybe I'll try some fuel injector cleaner before anything else, just to see what happens.  later...||||reg||1
